{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.6.6","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/rizviabiha/generativeai-vs-agenticai?scriptVersionId=369797474\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Generative AI vs Agentic AI – Understanding Through an HR Recruitment Example\n\n## 1. What is Generative AI?\n\n**Generative AI refers to a class of AI models that can create new content — such as text, images, audio, code, or video — that resembles human-created data.**\n\n### Traditional AI vs Generative AI\n\n- **Traditional AI:** It is about finding patterns in data and making predictions.\n- **Generative AI:** It is about learning the distribution of the data so that it can generate a new sample from it.\n\nIn simple words:\n\nTraditional AI focuses on predicting or classifying information, whereas Generative AI focuses on creating new content.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Understanding Generative AI Through an HR Recruitment Example\n\nLet's suppose I am an HR working in a company, and I need to hire a good backend engineer.\n\n## 2.1 Manual Recruitment Process\n\nFirst, I will have to:\n\n1. Create a Job Description (JD).\n2. Post the JD on different job platforms.\n3. Receive a large number of applications.\n4. Shortlist a few suitable applications.\n5. Conduct interviews with shortlisted candidates.\n6. Select a suitable candidate.\n7. Roll out the offer letter.\n8. Prepare the candidate's onboarding process.\n\nThis entire process is performed manually by the HR.\n\n## 2.2 Applying Generative AI to This Process\n\nNow, let's suppose my company provides me with an LLM-based chatbot.\n\nI will tell the chatbot about my goal:\n\n**Goal: I want to hire a backend engineer.**\n\nThen I will interact with the chatbot throughout the recruitment process.\n\n### Step 1: Creating a Job Description\n\nI will ask the chatbot:\n\n\"Can you draft a job description for a backend engineer?\"\n\nThe chatbot will generate a JD for me.\n\n### Step 2: Finding Job Platforms\n\nNext, I will ask:\n\n\"Which platforms can I use to post this job?\"\n\nThe chatbot will list some platforms where I can post the job.\n\nI will then visit those platforms and post the job myself.\n\n### Step 3: Shortlisting Candidates\n\nAfter receiving applications, I will ask the chatbot:\n\n\"How can I shortlist suitable candidates?\"\n\nIt might suggest that I look for candidates who have knowledge of cloud technologies and other relevant backend skills.\n\n### Step 4: Preparing for Interviews\n\nAfter shortlisting candidates, I will ask:\n\n\"What questions should I ask during the interview?\"\n\nThe chatbot will generate a list of interview questions.\n\n### Step 5: Preparing the Offer Letter\n\nAfter interviewing the candidates and selecting one, I will ask the chatbot to draft an offer letter.\n\nThe chatbot will generate the offer letter for me.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Problems with This Generative AI Approach\n\nAlthough Generative AI makes the recruitment process easier, this approach still has several limitations.\n\n### 1. Reactive Behaviour\n\nThe entire approach is reactive.\n\nThe chatbot only responds when I provide a prompt.\n\nIt has no independent understanding of the complete recruitment workflow and does not proactively move from one step to another.\n\nFor example, after generating the JD, it will not automatically post it on job platforms or start tracking applications.\n\nI have to give it instructions at every step.\n\n### 2. Lack of Memory\n\nThe chatbot does not necessarily retain the complete history and progress of the recruitment process.\n\nI may have to provide the same information repeatedly.\n\n### 3. Generic Responses\n\nThe chatbot gives generic responses because it does not have access to my company's specific knowledge.\n\nFor example, it does not necessarily know:\n\n- My company's hiring policies.\n- Our preferred candidate profiles.\n- Our recruitment best practices.\n- Our existing JD templates.\n\n### 4. Cannot Take Actions on Our Behalf\n\nThe chatbot can generate suggestions and content, but it cannot independently perform actions on external platforms.\n\nFor example, it cannot directly:\n- Post a job on LinkedIn.\n- Check applications.\n- Schedule interviews.\n- Send offer letters.\n\nI have to perform these actions myself.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Introducing RAG-Based Chatbots\n\nNow, suppose we want our chatbot to provide company-specific advice instead of generic responses.\n\nFor this, we will connect our chatbot to our company's specific knowledge base.\n\n### What Will We Provide to the Knowledge Base?\n\nWe can provide:\n\n1. Our company's hiring playbook.\n2. Best practices followed by our company for effective hiring.\n3. Past job description templates.\n4. Onboarding checklists.\n\nThis makes our chatbot aware of company-specific information.\n\n**This is called a RAG-based chatbot.**\n\nRAG stands for Retrieval-Augmented Generation.\n\n### How Does the RAG-Based Chatbot Help?\n\nNow, if we repeat the recruitment process, the chatbot can retrieve relevant information from our company's knowledge base and provide more context-specific responses.\n\nFor example:\n\n- It can generate a JD using our company's existing templates.\n- It can suggest hiring practices based on our company's playbook.\n- It can provide onboarding information using our existing checklists.\n\nThe process becomes easier because the chatbot now has access to our company's context.\n\n### But What Problems Still Remain?\n\nEven after implementing RAG, some limitations still exist:\n\n1. **It is still reactive:** The chatbot only responds when we give it a prompt.\n\n2. **It still lacks independent memory:** RAG provides access to relevant knowledge, but that does not automatically give the chatbot persistent memory of the ongoing recruitment workflow and its progress.\n\n3. **It cannot take actions:** It can provide company-specific suggestions, but it still cannot independently perform actions on external platforms.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Introducing Tool-Augmented Chatbots\n\nNow, what if we want our chatbot to take actions on our behalf?\n\nFor this, we can connect our chatbot with different tools and APIs.\n\n### Examples of Tools We Can Connect\n\n1. LinkedIn API\n2. Resume Parser\n3. Calendar API\n4. Mail API\n5. HRM Access Tool\n\nChatbots connected with such tools are called **Tool-Augmented Chatbots**.\n\n### How Does This Improve Our Recruitment Process?\n\nNow our chatbot can perform certain actions on our behalf.\n\nFor example:\n\n1. **Job Posting:** It can post a job on LinkedIn.\n\n2. **Application Tracking:** It can check how many applications have been received.\n\n3. **Resume Screening:** It can parse resumes and shortlist suitable candidates based on the given requirements.\n\n4. **Interview Scheduling:** It can use the Calendar API to schedule interviews.\n\n5. **Offer Letter:** It can organize and prepare offer letters.\n\nThe recruitment process becomes much easier because the chatbot can now interact with external tools and perform actions instead of just generating responses.\n\n### But What Problems Still Remain?\n\nEven though our chatbot can take actions, it still has some limitations.\n\n**1. It is still reactive.**\n\nIt performs actions only when we instruct it to do so.\n\n**2. It is not contextually aware of the complete workflow.**\n\nHaving access to tools does not automatically mean that the chatbot understands the entire recruitment process, its progress, and previous decisions.\n\n**3. It is not adaptive.**\n\nLet's understand this with an example.\n\nSuppose we posted a job on LinkedIn, but very few applications came in.\n\nA tool-augmented chatbot will not necessarily identify this problem and decide to take corrective action on its own.\n\nWe will have to ask:\n\n\"Check why we are receiving fewer applications.\"\n\nThe chatbot may then check our company's hiring playbook and suggest ways to improve the job posting.\n\nHowever, it will not independently recognize the situation, decide that a change is needed, and adapt its strategy without being instructed.\n\nTherefore, although it can perform actions, it is still not truly proactive or adaptive.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Introducing Agentic AI\n\nNow, what if we want our chatbot to do more than simply respond to prompts or execute individual instructions?\n\nWe want a system where:\n\n- We provide our goal.\n- It understands what needs to be achieved.\n- It creates a complete workflow.\n- It breaks the goal into smaller tasks.\n- It executes those tasks using available tools.\n- It monitors the progress.\n- It adapts when unexpected situations occur.\n- It provides updates repeatedly so that we can review its work.\n- It seeks human help or approval whenever necessary.\n\nThis is where **Agentic AI** comes into the picture.\n\n### What Is Different About Agentic AI?\n\nInstead of asking the chatbot to perform every individual step, we simply provide it with the overall goal.\n\nFor example:\n\n\"I want to hire a backend engineer.\"\n\nThe Agentic AI system can then:\n\n1. Create a complete recruitment workflow.\n2. Draft a JD using company-specific information.\n3. Post the JD on approved job platforms.\n4. Monitor incoming applications.\n5. Screen resumes according to the hiring requirements.\n6. Schedule interviews using the Calendar API.\n7. Prepare interview questions.\n8. Seek human approval at important checkpoints.\n9. Prepare the offer letter for the selected candidate.\n10. Organize the onboarding process.\n11. Monitor progress and adapt its approach when required.\n12. Provide regular updates for human review.\n\nFor example, if fewer applications are received than expected, the agent can recognize that the current approach is not producing the desired results, investigate the possible reasons, and modify its strategy within the permissions provided to it.\n\nIt does not have to wait for us to identify every problem and provide a separate prompt.\n\n**Agentic AI is proactive.**\n\n---\n\n# 7. Important Concepts to Remember\n\n### 1. Generative AI is a building block of Agentic AI.\n\nGenerative AI models, particularly LLMs, can be used as the reasoning and content-generation component of an Agentic AI system.\n\n### 2. Generative AI is a capability, whereas Agentic AI is a behaviour.\n\n- **Generative AI = Capability:** The ability to generate new content.\n- **Agentic AI = Behaviour:** The ability of a system to work towards a goal by planning, making decisions, taking actions, and adapting.\n\n### 3. RAG and Tools solve different problems.\n\n- **RAG:** Provides access to external knowledge and company-specific information.\n- **Tools:** Allow the system to interact with external applications and perform actions.\n\n### 4. Agentic AI brings multiple capabilities together.\n\nAn Agentic AI system can combine:\n- LLMs for generation and reasoning.\n- RAG for retrieving relevant knowledge.\n- Tools for interacting with external systems.\n- Memory for retaining relevant information.\n- Planning for breaking down goals.\n- Adaptability for handling changing situations.\n- Human-in-the-Loop for approval and supervision.\n\n---\n\n# 8. Overall Evolution: From Generative AI to Agentic AI\n\n| Approach | What It Can Do | Main Limitation |\n|---|---|---|\n| LLM-based Chatbot | Generate JDs, questions, suggestions, and offer letters. | Reactive, generic responses, no direct actions. |\n| RAG-based Chatbot | Generate company-specific responses using the company's knowledge base. | Still reactive and cannot independently execute actions. |\n| Tool-Augmented Chatbot | Interact with external tools and perform actions. | Does not necessarily have proactive behaviour, persistent workflow context, or adaptability. |\n| Agentic AI | Understand goals, plan workflows, use tools, monitor progress, and adapt. | Requires appropriate permissions, guardrails, and human oversight. |\n\n**Final Understanding:**\n\nGenerative AI helps an AI system create content.\n\nRAG helps it access relevant knowledge.\n\nTools help it perform actions.\n\nAgentic AI combines these capabilities to create a system that can independently work towards achieving a goal with minimal human guidance.\n\n**The fundamental shift is from asking AI to perform individual tasks to giving AI a goal and allowing it to work towards completing that goal.**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Agentic AI – Introduction and Key Characteristics\n\n## 1. What is Agentic AI?\n\nAgentic AI is a type of AI that can take up a task or goal from a user and then work toward completing it on its own with minimal human guidance.\n\nIt plans, takes actions, adapts to changes, and seeks help only when necessary.\n\nIn simple words, instead of just responding to a user's prompt, an Agentic AI system can independently work towards achieving a particular objective.\n\n### Key Characteristics of Agentic AI\n\n1. Autonomy\n2. Goal Orientation\n3. Planning\n4. Reasoning\n5. Adaptability\n6. Context Awareness\n\n---\n\n# 2. Key Characteristics of Agentic AI\n\n## 2.1 Autonomy\n\n**Definition:**\n\nAutonomy refers to the AI system's ability to make decisions and take actions on its own to achieve a given goal, without needing step-by-step human instructions.\n\nFor example:\n- Our AI recruiter is autonomous.\n- It's proactive.\n\n### Autonomy in Multiple Facets\n\nAutonomy can be understood through three different facets:\n\n**1. Execution**\n\nThe agent can independently execute actions required to accomplish its task.\n\n**2. Decision Making**\n\nThe agent can make decisions between available options without requiring human instructions at every step.\n\n**3. Tool Usage**\n\nThe agent can independently select and use available tools to complete its task.\n\n### Autonomy Can Be Controlled\n\nAlthough an agent can work independently, its autonomy should have certain boundaries.\n\nThere are four important ways to control autonomy:\n\n**a. Permission Scope**\n\n- Limits what tools or actions the agent can perform independently.\n- Example: The agent can screen candidates, but it needs approval before rejecting anyone.\n\n**b. Human-in-the-Loop (HITL)**\n\n- Insert checkpoints where human approval is required before the agent continues.\n- Example: The agent can prepare a job offer, but human approval is required before posting or sending it.\n\n**c. Override Controls**\n\n- Allow users to stop, pause, or change the agent's behaviour at any time.\n- Example: A pause screening command can halt the resume-screening process.\n\n**d. Guardrails / Policies**\n\n- Define hard rules or ethical boundaries that the agent must follow.\n- Example: Never schedule interviews on weekends.\n\n### Autonomy Can Be Dangerous\n\nGiving an AI agent too much autonomy can lead to unintended consequences.\n\nExamples:\n\n1. The application autonomously sends out job offers with incorrect salaries or terms.\n2. The application shortlists candidates by age or nationality, violating anti-discrimination laws.\n3. The application spends extra money on LinkedIn ads.\n\n**Important takeaway:** Autonomy is useful, but it must operate within clearly defined permissions, human approval checkpoints, override controls, and policies.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2 Goal Oriented\n\n**Definition:**\n\nBeing goal-oriented means that the AI system operates with a persistent objective in mind and continuously directs its actions to achieve that objective, rather than just responding to isolated prompts.\n\n### Important Characteristics of Goals\n\n1. **Goals act as a compass for autonomy.**\n\n   The goal gives direction to the agent's independent actions and decisions.\n\n2. **Goals can come with constraints.**\n\n   The agent must achieve its objective while respecting specified conditions and limitations.\n\n3. **Goals are stored in core memory.**\n\n   The agent needs to retain its main objective and relevant constraints throughout the task.\n\n4. **Goals can be altered.**\n\n   The objective can be modified when requirements or circumstances change.\n\n### Example: Goal Representation\n\nConsider an AI recruiting agent whose main goal is to hire a backend engineer.\n\nIts goal and constraints can be represented in a structured format:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"main_goal\": \"Hire a backend engineer\",\n  \"constraints\": {\n    \"experience\": \"2-4 years\",\n    \"remote\": true,\n    \"stack\": [\"Python\", \"Django\", \"Cloud\"]\n  },\n  \"status\": \"active\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2025-06-27\",\n  \"progress\": {\n    \"JD_created\": true,\n    \"posted_on\": [\"LinkedIn\", \"AngelList\"],\n    \"applications_received\": 8,\n    \"interviews_scheduled\": 2\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Understanding the example:**\n\n- `main_goal`: Defines what the agent needs to accomplish.\n- `constraints`: Defines the conditions that must be satisfied.\n- `status`: Indicates whether the goal is currently active.\n- `created_at`: Stores when the goal was created.\n- `progress`: Maintains information about the work completed so far.\n\nThe agent can use this information to understand its objective, constraints, and current progress.\n\n---\n\n## 2.3 Planning\n\n**Definition:**\n\nPlanning is the agent's ability to break down a high-level goal into a structured sequence of actions or subgoals and decide the best path to achieve the desired outcome.\n\nPlanning involves three major steps.\n\n### Step 1: Generating Multiple Candidate Plans\n\nThe agent generates different possible strategies to achieve the same goal.\n\nExample: The goal is to hire a backend engineer.\n\n- **Plan A:** Post the job description on LinkedIn, GitHub Jobs, and AngelList.\n- **Plan B:** Use internal referrals and hiring agencies.\n\nBoth plans aim to achieve the same objective but use different approaches.\n\n### Step 2: Evaluate Each Plan\n\nThe agent evaluates the available plans using different criteria.\n\n1. **Efficiency:** Which plan is faster?\n2. **Tool Availability:** Which tools are available to execute the plan?\n3. **Cost:** Does the plan require premium tools?\n4. **Risk:** Will the plan fail if there are no applicants?\n5. **Alignment with Constraints:** Does the plan satisfy the requirements, such as remote-only hiring or budget limitations?\n\n### Step 3: Select the Best Plan\n\nThe agent selects the most suitable plan with the help of:\n\n- **Human-in-the-Loop input:** Asking a human to choose between available options.\n\n  Example: \"Which of these options do you prefer?\"\n\n- **A pre-programmed policy:** Following predefined rules while selecting a plan.\n\n  Example: \"Favor low-cost channels first.\"\n\nThe selected plan is then used to guide the agent's execution.\n\n---\n\n## 2.4 Reasoning\n\n**Definition:**\n\nReasoning is the cognitive process through which an Agentic AI system interprets information, draws conclusions, and makes decisions — both while planning ahead and while executing actions in real time.\n\nReasoning is involved in two stages:\n\n### A. Reasoning During Planning\n\n1. **Goal Decomposition:** Break down abstract goals into concrete steps.\n\n2. **Tool Selection:** Decide which tools will be needed for which steps.\n\n3. **Resource Estimation:** Estimate time, dependencies, and risks.\n\n### B. Reasoning During Execution\n\n1. **Decision Making:** Choosing between available options.\n\n   Example: If three candidates match the requirements, the agent may schedule interviews with the two best candidates and reject the remaining one, subject to the required permissions.\n\n2. **HITL Handling:** Knowing when to pause and ask for human help.\n\n   Example: The agent is unsure about the salary range and needs human clarification.\n\n3. **Error Handling:** Interpreting tool/API failures and recovering from them.\n\n**Important takeaway:** Reasoning helps an agent decide what to do, which tools to use, how to handle decisions, and how to respond when something goes wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 2.5 Adaptability\n\n**Definition:**\n\nAdaptability is the agent's ability to modify its plans, strategies, or actions in response to unexpected conditions — all while staying aligned with the goal.\n\nAn agent should not blindly follow its original plan when circumstances change. It should be able to adjust its approach.\n\n### Examples of Adaptability\n\n1. **Failures (Calendar API)**\n\n   If the Calendar API fails, the agent needs to adjust its execution strategy instead of getting stuck.\n\n2. **External Feedback (Fewer applications)**\n\n   If the job posting receives fewer applications than expected, the agent can modify its approach to attract more candidates.\n\n3. **Changing Goals (Hiring a Freelancer)**\n\n   If the hiring requirement changes from hiring a full-time employee to hiring a freelancer, the agent should adapt its plan accordingly.\n\n**Important takeaway:** Adaptability allows an agent to handle unexpected situations and continue working towards its objective using an adjusted strategy.\n\n---\n\n## 2.6 Context Awareness\n\n**Definition:**\n\nContext awareness is the agent's ability to understand, retain, and utilize relevant information from the ongoing task, past interactions, user preferences, and environmental cues to make better decisions throughout a multi-step process.\n\nAn agent should not treat every action as an isolated task. It should consider the information collected throughout the entire process.\n\n### Types of Context\n\nConsider an AI recruiting agent that is responsible for hiring a backend engineer.\n\nThe agent may need the following context:\n\n**a. The Original Goal**\n\nThe agent needs to remember the original objective.\n\nExample: Hire a backend engineer.\n\n**b. Progress Till Now + Interaction History**\n\nThe agent should know what has already happened during the task.\n\nExample:\n- The job description was finalized.\n- The job description was posted on LinkedIn and GitHub Jobs.\n\n**c. Environment State**\n\nThe agent should consider the current situation and external conditions.\n\nExample:\n- Number of applicants so far = 8.\n- LinkedIn promotion ends in 2 days.\n\n**d. Tool Responses**\n\nThe agent should remember and utilize information returned by external tools.\n\nExamples:\n- Resume Parser → Candidate B has 3 years of Django + AWS experience.\n- Calendar API → No conflicts at 2 PM Wednesday.\n\n**e. User-Specific Preferences**\n\nThe agent should consider the user's preferences while making decisions.\n\nExamples:\n- Prefers remote-first candidates.\n- Likes receiving interview questions in a Google Doc.\n\n**f. Policies or Guardrails**\n\nThe agent must remember and follow the rules defined for the task.\n\nExamples:\n- Do not send an offer without explicit user approval.\n- Never use platforms that require paid ads unless approved.\n\n### Context Awareness Is Implemented Through Memory\n\nMemory helps the agent retain and use relevant information throughout a task.\n\nTwo important types of memory mentioned in the lecture are:\n\n**1. Short-Term Memory**\n\nStores information relevant to the current task and ongoing interaction.\n\n**2. Long-Term Memory**\n\nStores information that may remain useful across multiple tasks or future interactions.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Overall Revision\n\n| Characteristic | Main Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Autonomy | Allows the agent to act independently. |\n| Goal Orientation | Keeps the agent focused on a persistent objective. |\n| Planning | Breaks down a goal and identifies the best sequence of actions. |\n| Reasoning | Helps the agent interpret information and make decisions. |\n| Adaptability | Allows the agent to change its strategy when circumstances change. |\n| Context Awareness | Helps the agent understand and utilize relevant information from the task and previous interactions. |\n\n## 4. How These Characteristics Work Together\n\nConsider the example of an AI recruiting agent whose goal is to hire a backend engineer.\n\n1. **Goal Orientation:** The agent understands that its main objective is to hire a backend engineer with specific requirements.\n\n2. **Planning:** It generates possible strategies, such as posting job descriptions or using referrals.\n\n3. **Reasoning:** It evaluates the available strategies and decides which one is most suitable.\n\n4. **Autonomy:** It independently performs permitted actions, such as posting the job description and screening resumes.\n\n5. **Context Awareness:** It remembers the original requirements, candidate information, progress, user preferences, and tool responses.\n\n6. **Adaptability:** If the Calendar API fails or the hiring requirements change, it modifies its strategy accordingly.\n\nHuman-in-the-Loop checkpoints, permission scopes, override controls, and guardrails ensure that the agent operates within acceptable boundaries.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Final Understanding\n\nAgentic AI is not simply about making an AI model generate responses. It is about building a system that can work towards a goal.\n\nAn Agentic AI system:\n\n- Knows **what** it needs to achieve (Goal Orientation).\n- Decides **how** to achieve it (Planning).\n- Evaluates information and makes decisions (Reasoning).\n- Performs actions independently (Autonomy).\n- Adjusts its approach when situations change (Adaptability).\n- Remembers and uses relevant information (Context Awareness).\n\nAll these characteristics work together to help an AI system complete multi-step tasks with minimal human guidance.\n\n**One important distinction:** Autonomy does not mean unlimited freedom. An agent's independence must be controlled through permissions, human oversight, override mechanisms, and guardrails.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Agentic AI\n\n# 1. What Is Agentic AI?\n\n**Agentic AI is a type of AI that can take up a task or goal from a user and then work towards completing it on its own with minimal human guidance. It plans, takes actions, adapts to changes, and seeks help only when necessary.**\n\nUnlike traditional chatbots that simply respond to prompts, Agentic AI systems can independently decide what steps are required to achieve a given goal and execute those steps.\n\n# 2. Generative AI vs Traditional AI\n\n### Generative AI\n\n**Generative AI refers to a class of AI models that can create new content, such as text, images, audio, code, or video, that resembles human-created data.**\n\nGenerative AI learns the underlying distribution and patterns of data so that it can generate new samples from that distribution.\n\n### Traditional AI\n\n**Traditional AI is primarily about finding patterns in data and using those patterns to make predictions or decisions.**\n\n### Difference\n\n- Traditional AI focuses on analyzing data, identifying patterns, and making predictions.\n- Generative AI focuses on learning the distribution of data and generating new content.\n\n# 3. Understanding the Evolution Towards Agentic AI (Hiring Example)\n\nLet's understand how we move from a traditional manual hiring process to Agentic AI.\n\nSuppose I am an HR working in a company, and I need to hire a good backend engineer.\n\n## Step 1: Manual Hiring Process\n\nInitially, the entire hiring process is manual.\n\n1. Create a Job Description (JD).\n2. Post the JD on different job platforms.\n3. Receive multiple applications.\n4. Shortlist suitable candidates.\n5. Schedule and conduct interviews.\n6. Select the best candidate.\n7. Roll out the offer letter.\n8. Complete the candidate's onboarding.\n\nThis entire process requires human involvement at every stage.\n\n## Step 2: Using a Generative AI Chatbot (LLM-Based Chatbot)\n\nNow suppose our company provides us with an LLM-based chatbot.\n\nWe tell the chatbot our goal:\n\n\"I want to hire a backend engineer.\"\n\nWe can then interact with the chatbot to complete different stages of hiring.\n\n1. **Creating a JD:** Ask the chatbot to draft a job description for a backend engineer.\n2. **Finding platforms:** Ask which platforms we can use to post the job. It suggests platforms such as LinkedIn and other job portals.\n3. **Shortlisting candidates:** Ask what skills and qualifications we should look for. It might suggest candidates with knowledge of Python, cloud technologies, etc.\n4. **Preparing interview questions:** Ask the chatbot to generate interview questions for shortlisted candidates.\n5. **Preparing the offer letter:** Once a candidate is selected, ask the chatbot to draft an offer letter.\n\n### Problems with this approach\n\nAlthough Generative AI makes the process easier, there are some limitations:\n\n1. **Reactive:** The chatbot only responds when we provide a prompt. It has no independent understanding of the complete hiring workflow.\n\n2. **Lack of memory:** It may not retain the necessary information and progress throughout the entire hiring process.\n\n3. **Generic responses:** The chatbot provides generic suggestions because it does not know our company's specific hiring practices, requirements, and policies.\n\n4. **Cannot take actions:** It can suggest what to do, but it cannot independently perform external actions on our behalf.\n\n## Step 3: RAG-Based Chatbot (Company-Specific Knowledge)\n\nTo get company-specific advice, we connect our chatbot to our company's knowledge base.\n\nWe provide it with company-specific information, such as:\n\n- Hiring playbooks.\n- Company hiring best practices.\n- Previous JD templates.\n- Onboarding checklists.\n- Company-specific hiring guidelines.\n\nNow the chatbot can retrieve relevant information from our company's knowledge base using **RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)**.\n\nFor example, when we ask it to prepare a JD, it can refer to our previous JD templates and company hiring practices.\n\n### What improves?\n\n- Responses become company-specific.\n- Suggestions are based on our company's knowledge.\n- The chatbot understands our hiring practices and requirements better.\n\n### What problems still remain?\n\nEven after connecting the knowledge base:\n\n1. The chatbot is still reactive.\n2. It still does not independently manage the entire hiring workflow.\n3. It does not necessarily have persistent memory or progress tracking.\n4. It cannot independently perform external actions.\n\nRAG improves the knowledge available to the chatbot, but it does not automatically make the chatbot an autonomous agent.\n\n## Step 4: Tool-Augmented Chatbot\n\nNow suppose we want our chatbot to perform actions on our behalf.\n\nWe connect it with different tools and APIs, such as:\n\n- LinkedIn API.\n- Resume Parser.\n- Calendar API.\n- Email API.\n- HRM (Human Resource Management) access tools.\n\nNow our chatbot can perform actions such as:\n\n1. Post a job on LinkedIn.\n2. Check how many applications have been received.\n3. Parse resumes and shortlist candidates.\n4. Schedule interviews using the Calendar API.\n5. Organize and prepare offer letters.\n\nThis makes the hiring process easier because the chatbot can interact with external applications and perform actions.\n\n### What problems still remain?\n\nAlthough the chatbot can now use tools:\n\n1. It may still be reactive.\n2. It may not independently understand and manage the complete workflow.\n3. It may not adapt to unexpected situations on its own.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose our job posting receives very few applications.\n\nWe have to manually ask the chatbot:\n\n\"Why are we receiving so few applications? What should we do to improve the response?\"\n\nThe chatbot can check the company's hiring playbook and suggest improvements.\n\nHowever, it will not necessarily identify the problem, decide what to change, and take corrective action independently.\n\nTherefore, having tools does not automatically make a chatbot proactive or adaptive.\n\n## Step 5: Agentic AI Chatbot\n\nNow we introduce an Agentic AI chatbot.\n\nInstead of giving individual prompts for every step, we simply provide it with our goal:\n\n**\"Hire a backend engineer for our company.\"**\n\nThe Agentic AI system can:\n\n1. Understand and interpret our goal.\n2. Create a complete hiring workflow.\n3. Break the goal into smaller tasks.\n4. Decide which tools are required.\n5. Execute the tasks in the correct sequence.\n6. Monitor the progress of the hiring process.\n7. Adapt when unexpected situations arise.\n8. Ask for human approval whenever necessary.\n9. Provide regular updates so that we can review its work.\n\nFor example, if the job posting receives fewer applications than expected, the agent can recognize the problem, evaluate possible reasons, and decide what corrective steps to take within its permissions.\n\n**This is Agentic AI: a system that can understand a goal, plan a workflow, execute it, monitor progress, and adapt its actions to achieve the desired outcome.**\n\n### Important Takeaways\n\n- Agentic AI is proactive.\n- Generative AI is one of the building blocks of Agentic AI.\n- Generative AI is a capability, whereas Agentic AI is a behaviour.\n- RAG provides access to relevant knowledge.\n- Tools provide the ability to interact with external systems.\n- Memory helps maintain context and track progress.\n- An agent combines these capabilities to work towards a goal.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Key Characteristics of Agentic AI\n\n## 4.1 Autonomy\n\n**Autonomy refers to the AI system's ability to make decisions and take actions on its own to achieve a given goal, without needing step-by-step human instructions.**\n\nFor example, our AI recruiter can independently execute different stages of the hiring process.\n\n### Important aspects of Autonomy\n\n**1. Our AI recruiter is autonomous.**\n\nIt can perform multiple activities without requiring us to provide instructions for every individual step.\n\n**2. It is proactive.**\n\nIt does not always wait for a new prompt. It can monitor progress and take appropriate actions towards its goal.\n\n**3. Autonomy has multiple facets:**\n\n- **Execution:** Performing the required actions.\n- **Decision-making:** Choosing what action to take.\n- **Tool usage:** Selecting and using appropriate tools.\n\n**4. Autonomy can be controlled.**\n\nAutonomy does not mean that an AI agent should have unlimited freedom. We can control what it is allowed to do.\n\n- **Permission Scope:** Limits which tools or actions an agent can perform independently.\n\n  Example: The agent can screen candidates, but it needs approval before rejecting anyone.\n\n- **Human-in-the-Loop (HITL):** Inserts checkpoints where human approval is required before the agent continues.\n\n  Example: The agent can prepare an offer letter, but it must get approval before sending it.\n\n- **Override Controls:** Allow users to stop, pause, or change the agent's behaviour at any time.\n\n  Example: We can pause the screening process to stop further processing.\n\n- **Guardrails / Policies:** Define hard rules or ethical boundaries that the agent must follow.\n\n  Example: Never schedule interviews on weekends.\n\n**5. Autonomy can be dangerous.**\n\nIf an agent operates without proper restrictions, it can make incorrect or harmful decisions.\n\nExamples:\n\na. The application autonomously sends out job offers with incorrect salaries or terms.\n\nb. The application shortlists candidates based on age or nationality, violating anti-discrimination laws.\n\nc. The application spends extra money on LinkedIn advertisements.\n\n## 4.2 Goal-Oriented\n\n**Being goal-oriented means that the AI system operates with a persistent objective in mind and continuously directs its actions towards achieving that objective, rather than just responding to isolated prompts.**\n\n### Important aspects\n\n1. **Goals act as a compass for autonomy.**\n\n   The goal guides the agent's decisions and actions.\n\n2. **Goals can come with constraints.**\n\n   The agent must achieve the goal while respecting the given restrictions.\n\n3. **Goals are stored in core memory.**\n\n   The agent needs to maintain information about its main objective and relevant constraints.\n\n   Example:\n\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"main_goal\": \"Hire a backend engineer\",\n     \"constraints\": {\n       \"experience\": \"2-4 years\",\n       \"remote\": true,\n       \"stack\": [\"Python\", \"Django\", \"Cloud\"]\n     },\n     \"status\": \"active\",\n     \"created_at\": \"2025-06-27\",\n     \"progress\": {\n       \"JD_created\": true,\n       \"posted_on\": [\"LinkedIn\", \"AngelList\"],\n       \"applications_received\": 8,\n       \"interviews_scheduled\": 2\n     }\n   }\n   ```\n\n4. **Goals can be altered.**\n\n   If requirements change, the agent should be able to update its objective and continue accordingly.\n\n## 4.3 Planning\n\n**Planning is the agent's ability to break down a high-level goal into a structured sequence of actions or subgoals and decide the best path to achieve the desired outcome.**\n\nFor example, the goal is to hire a backend engineer.\n\n### Step 1: Generate multiple candidate plans\n\nThe agent can generate different approaches.\n\n- **Plan A:** Post the JD on LinkedIn, GitHub Jobs, and AngelList.\n- **Plan B:** Use internal referrals and hiring agencies.\n\n### Step 2: Evaluate each plan\n\nThe agent evaluates the available plans using different factors:\n\n- **Efficiency:** Which approach is faster?\n- **Tool Availability:** Which tools are available?\n- **Cost:** Does the approach require premium tools?\n- **Risk:** What happens if the approach fails?\n- **Alignment with Constraints:** Does the approach satisfy the requirements, such as remote-only hiring and budget?\n\n### Step 3: Select the best plan\n\nThe agent selects the most appropriate plan with the help of:\n\n- **Human-in-the-Loop input:** For example, asking, \"Which of these options do you prefer?\"\n- **Pre-programmed policies:** For example, \"Always prioritize low-cost channels first.\"\n\n## 4.4 Reasoning\n\n**Reasoning is the cognitive process through which an Agentic AI system interprets information, draws conclusions, and makes decisions — both while planning ahead and while executing actions in real time.**\n\n### Reasoning During Planning\n\n1. **Goal Decomposition:** Break down abstract goals into concrete steps.\n2. **Tool Selection:** Decide which tools will be needed for which steps.\n3. **Resource Estimation:** Estimate time, dependencies, and risks.\n\n### Reasoning During Execution\n\n1. **Decision-making:** Choosing between available options.\n\n   Example: If three candidates match the requirements, schedule interviews with the two best candidates and reject one, subject to the applicable approval policy.\n\n2. **HITL Handling:** Knowing when to pause and ask for human help.\n\n   Example: The agent is unsure about the expected salary range.\n\n3. **Error Handling:** Interpreting tool or API failures and recovering from them.\n\n## 4.5 Adaptability\n\n**Adaptability is the agent's ability to modify its plans, strategies, or actions in response to unexpected conditions — all while staying aligned with the goal.**\n\nExamples:\n\n1. **Failures (Calendar API):** If the Calendar API fails, the agent can try again or choose another permitted approach.\n\n2. **External Feedback:** If the job receives fewer applications than expected, the agent can reconsider its strategy.\n\n3. **Changing Goals:** If the company decides to hire a freelancer instead of a full-time employee, the agent can modify its plan.\n\n## 4.6 Context Awareness\n\n**Context awareness is the agent's ability to understand, retain, and utilize relevant information from the ongoing task, past interactions, user preferences, and environmental cues to make better decisions throughout a multi-step process.**\n\n### Types of Context\n\na. **The Original Goal:** The main objective the agent needs to achieve.\n\nb. **Progress Till Now + Interaction History:** What has already happened and what decisions have been made.\n\nExample: The JD was finalized and posted on LinkedIn and GitHub Jobs.\n\nc. **Environment State:** Information about the current situation.\n\nExample: The number of applicants so far is 8, or the LinkedIn promotion ends in two days.\n\nd. **Tool Responses:** Information received from external tools and APIs.\n\nExamples:\n- Resume Parser → Candidate B has 3 years of Django and AWS experience.\n- Calendar API → No conflicts at 2 PM on Wednesday.\n\ne. **User-Specific Preferences:** Preferences provided by the user.\n\nExamples:\n- Prefers remote-first candidates.\n- Likes receiving interview questions in a Google Doc.\n\nf. **Policies or Guardrails:** Rules the agent must follow.\n\nExamples:\n- Do not send an offer without explicit user approval.\n- Never use platforms that require paid advertisements unless approved.\n\n### How is Context Awareness Implemented?\n\nContext awareness is implemented through memory.\n\n1. Short-Term Memory\n2. Long-Term Memory\n\n---\n\n# 5. Components of Agentic AI\n\nAn Agentic AI system consists of five major components:\n\n1. Brain\n2. Orchestrator\n3. Tools\n4. Memory\n5. Supervisor\n\n## 5.1 Brain (LLM)\n\nThe brain of an Agentic AI system is generally an LLM.\n\nIt is responsible for understanding instructions, interpreting goals, reasoning, making decisions, and generating responses.\n\n### Responsibilities of the Brain\n\n| Function | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Goal Interpretation | Understands user instructions and translates them into objectives. |\n| Planning | Breaks down high-level goals into subgoals and ordered steps. |\n| Reasoning | Makes decisions, resolves ambiguity, and evaluates trade-offs. |\n| Tool Selection | Chooses which tool or tools to use at a given step. |\n| Communication | Generates natural-language outputs for humans or other agents. |\n\n## 5.2 Orchestrator\n\nThe orchestrator manages and coordinates the overall workflow of the agent.\n\nIt decides how different tasks should be executed, in what order they should happen, and what should happen when something goes wrong.\n\n### Responsibilities of the Orchestrator\n\n| Function | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Task Sequencing | Determines the order of actions (Step 1 → Step 2 → ...). |\n| Conditional Routing | Directs the workflow based on context (e.g., if failure, retry or escalate). |\n| Retry Logic | Handles failed tool calls or reasoning attempts with backoff. |\n| Looping & Iteration | Repeats steps (e.g., keeps checking job applications until 10 are received). |\n| Delegation | Decides whether to hand off work to tools, an LLM, or a human. |\n\n## 5.3 Tools\n\nTools allow the Agentic AI system to interact with external applications, services, and systems.\n\nThey extend the capabilities of the LLM beyond generating text.\n\n### Responsibilities of Tools\n\n**1. External Actions**\n\nPerform API calls.\n\nExamples:\n- Post a job.\n- Send an email.\n- Trigger onboarding.\n\n**2. Knowledge Base Access**\n\nRetrieve factual or domain-specific information using RAG or search tools to ground responses.\n\nExamples:\n- Retrieve company hiring policies.\n- Access previous JD templates.\n- Retrieve onboarding checklists.\n\n## 5.4 Memory\n\nMemory allows an Agentic AI system to retain relevant information, maintain context, and track progress throughout a task.\n\n### Types of Memory\n\n**1. Short-Term Memory**\n\nMaintains the active session's context.\n\nIt stores or maintains information such as:\n- Recent user messages.\n- Tool calls.\n- Immediate decisions.\n\n**2. Long-Term Memory**\n\nPersists important information across sessions.\n\nIt can retain:\n- High-level goals.\n- Past interactions.\n- User preferences.\n- Important decisions.\n\n**3. State Tracking**\n\nMonitors the progress of a task.\n\nIt keeps track of:\n- What has been completed.\n- What is still pending.\n- The current status of the workflow.\n\nExample:\n- JD posted.\n- Offer sent.\n\n## 5.5 Supervisor\n\nThe supervisor is responsible for monitoring the agent's behaviour and ensuring that it operates within defined rules and boundaries.\n\nIt helps maintain safety, control, and human oversight.\n\n### Responsibilities of the Supervisor\n\n| Function | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Approval Requests (HITL) | The agent checks with a human before performing high-risk actions (e.g., sending offers). |\n| Guardrails Enforcement | Blocks unsafe or non-compliant behaviour. |\n| Edge Case Escalation | Alerts humans when uncertainty or conflicts arise. |\n\n---\n\n# 6. Overall Understanding\n\nAn Agentic AI system combines different capabilities to achieve a goal:\n\n- **Brain:** Thinks, interprets, plans, and reasons.\n- **Orchestrator:** Coordinates the workflow and manages execution.\n- **Tools:** Allow the agent to interact with external systems.\n- **Memory:** Maintains context and tracks progress.\n- **Supervisor:** Ensures safety, approvals, and compliance.\n\nTogether, these components allow an AI system to move beyond simply responding to prompts and work towards completing goals autonomously.\n\n**Remember:**\n\nGenerative AI gives the system the capability to generate content. Agentic AI uses intelligence, planning, memory, tools, and orchestration to exhibit goal-directed behaviour and accomplish tasks.\n","metadata":{}}]}